window.NEO_FIELDKIT={"unit":"sets-and-venn-diagrams","expedition":"The Sorting Office","tools":[{"title":"Factor-pair card","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"12\" y=\"16\" width=\"76\" height=\"68\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f4f1e6\" stroke=\"#4a5a66\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f3a44\">1 × 12</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"58\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f3a44\">2 × 6</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f3a44\">3 × 4</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Find factors in pairs that multiply to the number.</b></p><p>Factors of 12: 1 × 12, 2 × 6, 3 × 4. So the factors are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12.</p><p>Stop when the pairs start to repeat.</p>","room":"The Letter Racks","lesson":"Lesson 1 · Sets, elements and set notation"},{"title":"And/or card","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"6\" y=\"14\" width=\"88\" height=\"72\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#f4f1e6\" stroke=\"#4a5a66\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"50\" r=\"24\" fill=\"rgba(47,109,176,.25)\" stroke=\"#2f6db0\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"50\" r=\"24\" fill=\"rgba(176,65,47,.22)\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/></svg>","body":"<p><b>“And” means both rules are true. “Or” means at least one is.</b></p><p>From 1 to 10, numbers that are odd <b>and</b> more than 6: 7 and 9.</p><p>Numbers that are odd <b>or</b> more than 6: 1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9 and 10.</p>","room":"The Parcel Bays","lesson":"Lesson 2 · Union, intersection and complement"},{"title":"Inequality line","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><path d=\"M8 56 H92\" stroke=\"#2f3a44\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M20 50V62M40 50V62M60 50V62M80 50V62\" stroke=\"#2f3a44\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"40\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2f6db0\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"40\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#2f6db0\" stroke=\"#2f6db0\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M26 40H74\" stroke=\"#2f6db0\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f3a44\">1</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f3a44\">4</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>&lt; means “less than”; ≤ means “less than or equal to”.</b></p><p>Whole numbers with 1 &lt; <i>n</i> ≤ 4 are 2, 3 and 4: not 1 (an open circle), but 4 is allowed (a filled circle).</p>","room":"The Address Desk","lesson":"Lesson 3 · Subsets, the empty set and sets defined algebraically"},{"title":"Two-way table guide","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"12\" y=\"18\" width=\"76\" height=\"64\" fill=\"#f4f1e6\" stroke=\"#4a5a66\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M12 40H88M12 61H88M38 18V82M63 18V82\" stroke=\"#4a5a66\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"63\" y=\"40\" width=\"25\" height=\"21\" fill=\"rgba(242,193,78,.6)\"/></svg>","body":"<p><b>Find the row, then the column. The cell where they meet is the count.</b></p><p>The row total counts everything in that row, whatever its column.</p>","room":"The Three-Chute Machine","lesson":"Lesson 4 · Three-set Venn diagrams"},{"title":"Chance card","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><path d=\"M30 26 Q50 10 70 26 L78 84 H22 Z\" fill=\"#c9b48a\" stroke=\"#6b5a40\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"34\" y=\"44\" width=\"12\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#b0412f\"/><rect x=\"54\" y=\"44\" width=\"12\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#2f6db0\"/><rect x=\"44\" y=\"62\" width=\"12\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#2f6db0\"/></svg>","body":"<p><b>When every outcome is equally likely:</b></p><p>probability = <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;vertical-align:middle;text-align:center;line-height:1.1\"><span style=\"border-bottom:2px solid currentColor;padding:0 3px\">number of outcomes you want</span><span style=\"padding:0 3px\">total number of outcomes</span></span></p><p>A bag has 2 green and 7 yellow counters. P(green) = <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;vertical-align:middle;text-align:center;line-height:1.1\"><span style=\"border-bottom:2px solid currentColor;padding:0 3px\">2</span><span style=\"padding:0 3px\">9</span></span>.</p>","room":"The Dispatch Board","lesson":"Lesson 5 · Venn diagram problems, including a first link to probability"}]};
